      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the area of the shaded region in the given figure. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the coordinates of the points:**\n   - Point \\( A \\) is at \\((0, 0)\\).\n   - Point \\( B \\) is at \\((4, 0)\\).\n   - Point \\( C \\) is at \\((4, 4)\\).\n   - Point \\( D \\) is the midpoint of arc \\( ADB \\), so it is at \\((2, 0)\\).\n   - Point \\( E \\) is the midpoint of arc \\( BEC \\), so it is at \\((3, 2)\\).\n   - Point \\( F \\) is the midpoint of arc \\( DFE \\), so it is at \\((2, 2)\\).\n\n2. **Determine the radius of the semicircle \\( DFE \\):**\n   - The coordinates of \\( D \\) are \\((2, 0)\\).\n   - The coordinates of \\( E \\) are \\((3, 2)\\).\n   - The coordinates of \\( F \\) are \\((2, 2)\\).\n   - The radius of the semicircle \\( DFE \\) is the distance between \\( D \\) and \\( E \\), which is the same as the distance between \\( D \\) and \\( F \\). Using the distance formula:\n     \\[\n     DE = \\sqrt{(3-2)^2 + (2-0)^2} = \\sqrt{1 + 4} = \\sqrt{5}\n     \\]\n     The radius of the semicircle \\( DFE \\) is \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of the semicircle \\( DFE \\):**\n   - The area of a full circle with radius \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\) is \\( \\pi \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\pi \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} = \\frac{5\\pi}{4} \\).\n   - The area of the semicircle \\( DFE \\) is half of the area of the full circle:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of } DFE = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5\\pi}{4} = \\frac{5\\pi}{8}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the area of the square \\( ABCD \\):**\n   - The side length of the square \\( ABCD \\) is 4 units (the distance between \\( A \\) and \\( B \\)).\n   - The area of the square \\( ABCD \\) is \\( 4 \\times 4 = 16 \\).\n\n5. **Calculate the area of the semicircle \\( ADBE \\):**\n   - The diameter of the semicircle \\( ADBE \\) is the distance between \\( A \\) and \\( B \\), which is 4 units. So, the radius is \\( \\frac{4}{2} = 2 \\).\n   - The area of a full circle with radius 2 is \\( \\pi \\cdot 2^2 = 4\\pi \\).\n   - The area of the semicircle \\( ADBE \\) is half of the area of the full circle:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of } ADBE = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4\\pi = 2\\pi\n     \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the area of the shaded region:**\n   - The shaded region is the area of the semicircle \\( DFE \\) minus the area of the overlapping part with the semicircle \\( ADBE \\).\n   - The area of the semicircle \\( DFE \\) is \\( \\frac{5\\pi}{8} \\).\n   - The area of the semicircle \\( ADBE \\) is \\( 2\\pi \\).\n   - The overlapping area is the sum of the areas of the two quarter circles formed by the intersection of the semicircles \\( DFE \\) and \\( ADBE \\). Each of these quarter circles has a radius of \\( \\sqrt{4^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{16 - 4} = \\sqrt{12} = 2\\sqrt{3} \\).\n   - The area of one quarter circle is \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\pi (2\\sqrt{3})^2 = \\frac{1}{4} \\pi \\cdot 12 = 3\\pi \\).\n   - There are two such quarter circles, so the total overlapping area is \\( 2 \\cdot 3\\pi = 6\\pi \\).\n   - The area of the shaded region is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of shaded region} = \\frac{5\\pi}{8} - 6\\pi + \\frac{5\\pi}{8} = 6\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{8} = \\frac{48\\pi}{8} - \\frac{5\\pi}{8} = \\frac{43\\pi}{8}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{43\\pi}{8}}\\).",
